# First Thread Workflow

This walkthrough focuses on daily thread operations in the CLI.

## 1. Start a thread and capture context

```bash
loom
```

Inside the REPL, give direct instructions and include local repo context in your prompt.

## 2. Exit and verify thread persistence

Press `Ctrl+C` to leave the REPL, then list threads:

```bash
loom list
```

## 3. Resume work

```bash
loom resume
```

Or reopen an exact thread:

```bash
loom resume <thread-id>
```

## 4. Search prior work

Use full-text and metadata-aware search:

```bash
loom search "wireguard setup"
loom search "main" --limit 50
loom search "abcdef1" --json
```

## 5. Control thread visibility

For synced threads, set visibility explicitly:

```bash
loom share --visibility organization
loom share <thread-id> --visibility public
loom share <thread-id> --support
```

### Visibility model

- `organization`: visible to organization members
- `private`: synced but private to your account
- `public`: publicly accessible
- `--support`: grants support-team access

<Callout variant="warning" title="Private session caveat">
	`loom private` sessions are local-only and cannot be shared later.
</Callout>

## 6. Use a private thread when required

```bash
loom private
```

Private mode is for sensitive work that must not sync to server-side storage.

## Common patterns

<Steps>
	<Step title="Long-running change session">
		Start with `loom`, pause with `Ctrl+C`, resume with `loom resume` as needed.
	</Step>
	<Step title="Support escalation">
		Run `loom share --support` on the active thread to grant support visibility.
	</Step>
	<Step title="Research recall">
		Use `loom search` to recover earlier decisions and commit references.
	</Step>
</Steps>
